WEBVTT
Kind: captions
Language: en

00:00:00.000 --> 00:00:02.640
Here we go again. I'm back to talk about a core set of

00:00:02.640 --> 00:00:05.655
concepts in marketing: segmentation and positioning.

00:00:05.655 --> 00:00:07.770
Segmentation is the process of finding and

00:00:07.770 --> 00:00:10.560
grouping customers based on their needs or characteristics.

00:00:10.560 --> 00:00:13.080
For instance, there may be customers who care about

00:00:13.080 --> 00:00:16.065
prestige and others who care more about practicality.

00:00:16.065 --> 00:00:19.650
These would belong to two different segments because their tastes are different.

00:00:19.650 --> 00:00:22.710
A lot of times when people think of segments they characterize

00:00:22.710 --> 00:00:26.640
the difference between people as being about their age or socioeconomic status.

00:00:26.640 --> 00:00:29.895
This is particularly common in the media industry which refers to quote,

00:00:29.895 --> 00:00:32.145
"appeals to a certain demographic."

00:00:32.145 --> 00:00:34.710
The problem with this form of segmentation is

00:00:34.710 --> 00:00:37.270
the demographic characteristics typically

00:00:37.270 --> 00:00:40.535
don't do a good job of helping us predict what people will like.

00:00:40.535 --> 00:00:43.240
Sure, there are products like diapers,

00:00:43.240 --> 00:00:45.500
and those cases, knowing your baby is

00:00:45.500 --> 00:00:48.155
a pretty damn good predictor of whether you'll be consuming them.

00:00:48.155 --> 00:00:49.820
But for most other products,

00:00:49.820 --> 00:00:53.455
knowing your age or where you were born, is hardly predictive.

00:00:53.455 --> 00:00:58.055
So ideally you should segment based on customer preferences and attitudes.

00:00:58.055 --> 00:01:02.210
The tricky part is that these are harder to observe than age or gender.

00:01:02.210 --> 00:01:05.225
But these are the key predictors of behavior.

00:01:05.225 --> 00:01:08.360
Positioning is what you do to explain to

00:01:08.360 --> 00:01:12.110
your target customer that your product meets their needs best.

00:01:12.110 --> 00:01:15.000
This can be done by advertising, pricing,

00:01:15.000 --> 00:01:18.565
branding, and even based on the features you put in your product.

00:01:18.565 --> 00:01:20.410
It's where the rubber meets the road,

00:01:20.410 --> 00:01:23.270
where your customer sees that your product meets

00:01:23.270 --> 00:01:27.965
their needs so well that they're willing to part with their hard-earned money to get you.

00:01:27.965 --> 00:01:30.095
Positioning is critical.

00:01:30.095 --> 00:01:33.530
The same product can be positioned in two different ways and as

00:01:33.530 --> 00:01:38.075
a result can either appeal or fail to appeal to a mass market.

00:01:38.075 --> 00:01:41.750
The traffic and navigation mobile app Waze for example,

00:01:41.750 --> 00:01:45.815
started out with a business model much different than what they're known for today.

00:01:45.815 --> 00:01:48.500
Originally, their plan was to create

00:01:48.500 --> 00:01:52.025
inexpensive maps by crowdsourcing information from drivers.

00:01:52.025 --> 00:01:55.640
At the time, maps were expensive to produce and to purchase,

00:01:55.640 --> 00:01:57.970
and Waze was going to provide a cheaper

00:01:57.970 --> 00:02:02.105
up-to-date and easy to update alternative to existing competitors.

00:02:02.105 --> 00:02:05.360
The positioning was around inexpensive navigation.

00:02:05.360 --> 00:02:07.115
As it was building its product,

00:02:07.115 --> 00:02:09.350
Google launched Google Maps,

00:02:09.350 --> 00:02:11.030
which gave away its maps for free.

00:02:11.030 --> 00:02:14.615
Waze could no longer hope to charge for map creation.

00:02:14.615 --> 00:02:17.690
Google's move could have spelled the end for Waze,

00:02:17.690 --> 00:02:19.805
its founders, and its employees.

00:02:19.805 --> 00:02:24.680
Instead, the company repositioned from navigation to commuting.

00:02:24.680 --> 00:02:29.300
What's the difference? Well, navigation is when we go to places we don't know.

00:02:29.300 --> 00:02:32.030
Commuting is when we go to places that we do know.

00:02:32.030 --> 00:02:35.165
We don't need a map to help us find our destination.

00:02:35.165 --> 00:02:36.875
We need a map product,

00:02:36.875 --> 00:02:39.020
to help us get there quickly.

00:02:39.020 --> 00:02:43.520
No other product offered this benefit as well as Waze did in the marketplace.

00:02:43.520 --> 00:02:45.055
The rest is history.

00:02:45.055 --> 00:02:49.940
Waze gained tens of millions of users and was sold to Google for over a billion dollars.

00:02:49.940 --> 00:02:52.655
Segmentation is the basis of product success

00:02:52.655 --> 00:02:55.445
because it's at the heart of understanding consumer needs.

00:02:55.445 --> 00:02:57.125
Without meeting consumer need,

00:02:57.125 --> 00:02:58.235
a product fails.

00:02:58.235 --> 00:02:59.585
Plain and simple.

00:02:59.585 --> 00:03:02.180
But doing a thorough analysis isn't enough.

00:03:02.180 --> 00:03:07.520
You also need your targeted segment to perceive you as the solution to their problem.

00:03:07.520 --> 00:03:09.710
This is where positioning comes into play.

00:03:09.710 --> 00:03:13.090
In our course, we discussed each of these concepts in depth.

